= Knowledge management — Simple =

'''Knowledge management''' is the organized practice of helping people create, find, share, preserve, improve, and use [[Knowledge]].

It involves people, work processes, rules, and technology. A folder full of documents is not enough.

A useful knowledge-management system helps people answer questions such as:

* What do we already know?
* Where did this answer come from?
* Who has relevant experience?
* Is this still current?
* What evidence supports it?
* Who is responsible for updating it?

== Explicit and tacit knowledge ==

Some knowledge is easy to record explicitly:

* manuals;
* source code;
* checklists;
* datasets;
* standards.

Other knowledge is difficult to write down completely. An experienced technician may recognize a failure from a subtle sound, or a scientist may know how to adjust an experiment after years of practice.

This is often called '''tacit knowledge'''. Mentoring, observation, practice, and communities can be important when documents are not enough.

== Creating and sharing knowledge ==

Knowledge management can include:

* documenting lessons from projects;
* maintaining procedures;
* recording why important decisions were made;
* making experts easy to find;
* training new members;
* sharing examples;
* reviewing old material;
* correcting errors.

Ikujiro Nonaka's influential SECI model describes interactions between tacit and explicit knowledge.<ref name="nonaka">[https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.5.1.14 Nonaka, I. (1994), “A Dynamic Theory of Organizational Knowledge Creation.”]</ref> The model is widely used but has also been criticized, so it should not be treated as a universal law of how knowledge is created.<ref name="gourlay">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-6486.2006.00637.x Gourlay, S. (2006), “Conceptualizing Knowledge Creation: A Critique of Nonaka's Theory.”]</ref>

== Documents versus people ==

Two common strategies are:

'''Codification''' — put reusable knowledge into documents, databases, or structured systems.

'''Personalization''' — help people find and work with knowledgeable people.

Both are useful.

A written troubleshooting guide can solve common problems quickly. A rare and complicated problem may still require an expert.

== Knowledge needs maintenance ==

Old knowledge can be dangerous.

A good system records:

* who created something;
* when it was reviewed;
* what evidence supports it;
* which version is current;
* where it applies;
* when it should be reviewed again.

Obsolete information should be retired or clearly marked instead of remaining beside current guidance.

== Technology ==

Knowledge-management technology can include wikis, search engines, document systems, expert directories, version control, knowledge graphs, and AI tools.

Technology can make knowledge easier to find, but it cannot guarantee that the content is correct.

A review of knowledge-management systems emphasizes creation, transfer, and application rather than technology alone.<ref name="alavi">[https://aisel.aisnet.org/misq/vol25/iss1/6/ Alavi and Leidner (2001), “Knowledge Management and Knowledge Management Systems.”]</ref>

== Artificial intelligence ==

AI can help search, summarize, classify, or draft knowledge.

But generated answers can also be wrong. AI-assisted knowledge management should preserve sources and distinguish retrieved evidence from generated text.

== Measuring success ==

Counting documents is a poor measure of success.

Better questions include:

* Can users find the right answer?
* Is the answer correct?
* How quickly are errors corrected?
* Can new staff learn faster?
* Are people repeatedly solving the same problem?
* Does important knowledge disappear when experts leave?

